# Independent audit of the full Construction 8/9 parameter families

10 October 2026. **Pass for the complete parameterized existence assertions stated in the companion**, together with its firmness, thickness, residual connectedness and flag-transitivity conclusions. This supplements the frozen [core audit](phase7_suzuki_independent_audit.md); it does not modify that audit's proof version or extend a claim of exhaustive classification. Exact versions are recorded in [the companion audit record](phase7_suzuki_families_audit.json).

I checked the parameter clauses of L110 Constructions 8 and 9 on printed pp. 17–18 against the local primary full text. The number of chosen relative field primes is k=alpha−1. The source restriction alpha≤Omega(n) therefore forces Omega(e)≥1 after removing those k distinct prime factors, so the odd base degree e is at least three. This matters twice: the terminal subfield group is simple, and each nonsplit base order gamma is greater than one. The proof does not silently include the separate base-two construction.

The second torus is correctly obtained from the binary order-five element c=r²w. Its degree-four irreducible polynomial follows from ord_5(2)=4, and its four distinct eigenlines give a maximal symplectic torus. Since rho fixes c, it preserves that torus. Its five rho-fixed points select trace −2 in the same character-lattice calculation as the core argument. Conjugation by the binary r normalizes the torus and supplies the order-four complement in all odd extensions. These steps provide the companion order sequence, rather than merely assuming that both signs can be chosen independently in different fields.

Branch alignment is sound. The two nonsplit orders at each degree are coprime. The branch chosen at the base is already divisible by gamma in every containing field, and gamma>1 excludes divisibility by gamma in the other branch. Thus every source-admissible numerator and the ambient divisibility condition select this same compatible torus family. The split construction uses the standard diagonal family; the stated congruence for the Suzuki field exponent verifies compatibility of its matrix convention under restriction.

The valuation argument covers the delicate case in which a selected prime ell_i divides gamma. Its valuation in m is exactly v_ell_i(gamma)+1, because selected primes are distinct. If its relative field type is removed, the strong gcd identity and divisibility of the smaller torus order force the remaining field's valuation to be exactly the base valuation. Thus exactly one extra copy is removed, even if several copies were already present in the base. Other selected factors and unselected base factors have the asserted valuations. The equation gcd(e a_i,n_J)=e remains valid when a_i itself divides e.

For Construction 8, the individual divisibilities suffice for the ambient cyclic subgroup: the least common multiple of the numbers gamma ell_i is gamma times their product, since the ell_i are distinct. For Construction 9 this divisibility is retained directly from the source. No extra unmentioned coprimality assumption is needed in either case.

The coordinate argument is also correct with overlapping primes. The map from cyclic A to the product of its prime-order quotients is surjective by the ordinary Chinese remainder theorem; its kernel has order gamma. Passing from A/A_i to K/H_i is a bijection of sets because the common complement is contained in H_i. It does not turn the generally nonnormal H_i into quotient-group kernels. The core attachment lemma therefore applies to every admissible parameter choice.

The maximality and connectivity cases cover all proper flag subsets and all residues of rank at least two. For cyclic-by-complement residues, the least common multiple of two different cyclic deletion orders is the full cyclic order, including overlapping-base cases. The remaining cases use the retained maximality of simple prime-degree subfields and full torus normalizers. All panel indices are odd primes or proper subgroup indices greater than two in a simple group. Hence thickness follows.

I inspected the new checker and reran a byte-identical temporary copy with its five bound dependencies. The complete output matches the original except for timing: the order-five seed, its five-element centralizer, all subset intersections in the three overlapping-prime examples, and every cyclic coordinate tuple pass. See [the independent run](phase7_suzuki_families_audit_run.json). These finite checks corroborate the delicate steps; the parameter-uniform conclusion is supplied by the written valuation, descent and residue proofs.

No mathematical gap was found. This is internal independent review, retaining classical Suzuki torus/subfield theory and Lang–Steinberg through the core note. It is not a classification of arbitrary embeddings, a proof of geometric intersection-property axioms, a full Lean formalization, external peer review, or a publication-priority claim.
